The framebuffer's buffer belongs to the framebuffer, not to the open file

The vendor's DRM framebuffer emulation frees the framebuffer's buffer
when the last user closes /dev/fb0. That is wrong in two ways, and both
of them show up on the television.

The plane may still be reading it. The plane's address is programmed
when the framebuffer's contents are committed, and nothing undoes that
when the file is closed, so the display went on reading memory that had
been handed back to the allocator.

The next open has to find it at the SAME address. Because the buffer
was freed, the next open allocated a new one - usually at a different
address - while the plane still pointed at the old one. The display
therefore showed the OLD buffer, which is what "the display shows a
stale frame" looks like from the outside, while the client that had
just drawn into the new one saw nothing.

What this patch does:

  * the buffer is unmapped when the file is closed, but not freed: it
    belongs to the framebuffer, and it is freed when the framebuffer
    goes away;

  * a buffer that is being replaced - because the geometry changed -
    is retired rather than freed, because the plane may still be
    reading it. The plane's address is only recomputed on a commit, so
    the old buffer is kept until the framebuffer goes away;

  * the framebuffer's own destroy is not allowed to touch the buffer
    a second time;

  * the "force_free_mem" debug attribute now takes the plane off the
    display before the memory goes back to the allocator;

  * the dma-buf reference is taken when the buffer is mapped, so that
    every unmap has one to give back.

The framebuffer's buffer was also never actually freed by the old code
- the check for "somebody else still has it mapped" always found the
mapping the mode set had taken, so the buffer leaked. It is freed now.
